ations during the Birmingham campaign, while also incorporating investigative journalism elements regarding historical events.","method":"llm","model":"gpt-4o-mini","reviewStatus":"generated","inputHash":"8b318389d1be68237e87103fc7d81d67116641aa285f3bbd097088336afe86ef","summary":"Carry Me Home: Birmingham, Alabama, the Climactic Battle of the Civil Rights Revolution, is a nonfiction book about the African-American civil rights movement written by Diane McWhorter and published by Simon & Schuster in 2001. The book, which is part investigative journalism and part memoir, won the J. Anthony Lukas Book Prize and the 2002 Pulitzer Prize for General Nonfiction. McWhorter grew up in Birmingham, Alabama, and recounts being about the same age as the girls killed in the September 1963 bombing of the 16th Street Baptist Church, though she \"was growing up on the wrong side of the revolution\". While four black girls were murdered in that day's bombing, McWhorter recalls that the only repercussion of the killings on her white high school was the cancellation of a play rehearsal. Carry Me Home describes how bigotry was prevalent among whites and her interviews and reviews of documents from the civil rights era showed \"the long tradition of enmeshment between law enforcers and Klansmen\", ranging from local and state police to agents of the Federal Bureau of Investigation.","displaySummary":"The book, which is part investigative journalism and part memoir, won the J. Anthony Lukas Book Prize and the 2002 Pulitzer Prize for General Nonfiction. McWhorter grew up in Birmingham, Alabama, and recounts being about the same age as the girls killed in the September 1963 bombing of the 16th Street Baptist Church, though she \"was growing up on the wrong side of the revolution\". While four black girls were murdered in that day's bombing, McWhorter recalls that the only repercussion of the killings on her white high school was the cancellation of a play rehearsal. Carry Me Home describes how bigotry was prevalent among whites and her interviews and reviews of documents from the civil rights era showed \"the long tradition of enmeshment between law enforcers and Klansmen\", ranging from local and state police to agents of the Federal Bureau of Investigation.","subjectCategories":[{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Race relations","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-carry-me-home-birmingham-alabama-the-climactic-battle-of-the-civil-rights-movement-diane-mcwhort"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"History","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-carry-me-home-birmingham-alabama-the-climactic-battle-of-the-civil-rights-movement-diane-mcwhort"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"African Americans","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-carry-me-home-birmingham-alabama-the-climactic-battle-of-the-civil-rights-movement-diane-mcwhort"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Civil rights movements","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-carry-me-home-birmingham-alabama-the-climactic-battle-of-the-civil-rights-movement-diane-mcwhort"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Civil rights","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-carry-me-home-birmingham-alabama-the-climactic-battle-of-the-civil-rights-movement-diane-mcwhort"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Civil Rights Movement","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-carry-me-home-birmingham-alabama-the-climactic-battle-of-the-civil-rights-movement-diane-mcwhort"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Ethnische Beziehungen","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-carry-me-home-birmingham-alabama-the-climactic-battle-of-the-civil-rights-movement-diane-mcwhort"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Bürgerrechtsbewegung","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-carry-me-ho